{"type":"message","id":"41d95ea4","parentId":"0de85b56","timestamp":"2026-02-01T03:26:57.244Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01EqjzHCQLNYGp3qr3xrhQ4S","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/home/ec2-user/clawd/projects/lebbie-chocolates/BUSINESS-PLAN.md","content":"# Lebbie's Luxury Chocolates — Business Plan\n\n## Executive Summary\nLebbie's Luxury Chocolates is a premium artisanal bon bon brand rooted in Sierra Leonean single-origin cocoa, handcrafted in Baltimore by a European-trained chocolatier with a powerful personal story. The brand combines world-class technique (FBM Boscolo Milan + Ferrandi Paris), ethically sourced beans with direct ties to West African cocoa communities, and a founder narrative that includes 5 years of humanitarian work building hospitals with Partners In Health.\n\n---\n\n## Market Analysis\n\n### The Opportunity\n- US premium chocolate market: **$23B+**, growing 8-10% annually\n- Bean-to-bar / artisanal segment growing **15-20%** YoY\n- Consumers increasingly pay premiums for: origin story, ethical sourcing, small-batch craft\n- Single-origin African cocoa is under-represented vs. South American origins — white space\n\n### Target Customer\n- **Primary:** Gift buyers (corporate + personal), ages 28-55, $75K+ HHI\n- **Secondary:** Chocolate enthusiasts, foodies, ethical consumers\n- **Tertiary:** Baltimore locals, PIH/NGO community, diaspora communities\n\n### Competitive Landscape\n- **Godiva/Lindt:** Mass luxury — no origin story, no craft credibility\n- **Local bean-to-bar** (Charm City Chocolate, etc.): Craft but lack European pedigree + Africa story\n- **Direct competitors:** Almost none combine Sierra Leonean origin + Milan/Paris training + humanitarian backstory\n\n---\n\n## Unique Selling Proposition\n\n**\"From the cocoa farms of Sierra Leone to the pastry schools of Europe to your doorstep in Baltimore.\"**\n\nThree pillars no competitor can replicate:\n1. **Origin:** Direct relationship with Sierra Leonean cocoa farmers (5 years living in-country)\n2. **Craft:** Trained at FBM Boscolo (Milan) and Ferrandi (Paris) — two of Europe's finest\n3. **Purpose:** Every purchase supports awareness of West African cocoa communities; founder built hospitals with PIH\n\n---\n\n## Product Line\n\n### Collections (4 signature lines)\n| Collection | Theme | Price Point (box of 9) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Salone Collection** | Tropical fruits, palm wine, ginger, coconut | $38 |\n| **Paris Collection** | Lavender, champagne, crème brûlée, rose | $42 |\n| **Milano Collection** | Espresso, amaretto, hazelnut, gianduja | $42 |\n| **Charm City Collection** | Old Bay caramel, Berger cookie, black walnut, honey | $38 |\n\n### Gift Boxes\n- **The Grand Tour** (16 pc, all collections): $65\n- **Corporate Gift Box** (25 pc, branded): $85\n- **Subscription:** Monthly box of 9, $36/mo\n\n---\n\n## Revenue Model\n\n### Channels (Year 1 priorities)\n1. **Direct-to-consumer website** (40% of revenue) — highest margin\n2. **Baltimore farmers markets** (25%) — brand building + cash flow\n3. **Corporate gifting** (20%) — holiday season heavy\n4. **Local retail consignment** (15%) — select shops, restaurants\n\n### Pricing & Margins\n- COGS per bon bon: ~$1.20 (cocoa, fillings, packaging, labor)\n- Retail price per bon bon: ~$4.25 average\n- **Gross margin: ~72%**\n- After packaging, shipping, platform fees: **Net margin ~45-55%**\n\n---\n\n## Startup Costs Estimate\n\n| Item | Cost |\n|---|---|\n| Commercial kitchen rental (shared, first 3 months) | $1,800 |\n| Tempering machine + molds + tools | $2,500 |\n| Initial cocoa bean inventory (50 lbs) | $600 |\n| Packaging (boxes, inserts, branded materials) | $1,200 |\n| Website + e-commerce setup | $0 (Doug builds it) |\n| Food handler permits + cottage food license (MD) | $300 |\n| Farmers market booth fees (season) | $500 |\n| Photography (Doug does his own — $0) | $0 |\n| **Total startup** | **~$6,900** |\n\n---\n\n## Path to $2,000/month from Chocolate\n\n### Month 1-2: Foundation\n- Secure MD cottage food or shared kitchen license\n- Perfect 4 collections (16 recipes)\n- Launch website with waitlist → convert to orders\n- Photograph everything (Doug's photography skills = free world-class product shots)\n\n### Month 3-4: Farmers Markets + Local\n- 2 markets/week in Baltimore (Fells Point, Baltimore Farmers Market)\n- Target: 40-60 bon bons/market day = $170-250/day\n- Monthly from markets alone: $1,400-2,000\n\n### Month 5-6: Online + Corporate\n- Launch DTC e-commerce (Shopify or custom)\n- Instagram content machine (photographer + chocolatier = unfair advantage)\n- First corporate gifting outreach (holiday season prep)\n- Target: 15-20 online orders/month @ $50 avg = $750-1,000\n\n### Month 6+: $2K/month target\n- Markets: $1,200/mo (2x/week, conservative)\n- Online DTC: $500/mo\n- Corporate/wholesale: $300/mo\n- **Total: $2,000/mo** ✓\n\n---\n\n## Feeding the $15K/month Goal\n\nChocolate is **one pillar** of a diversified income strategy:\n\n| Stream | Monthly Target | Timeline |\n|---|---|---|\n| Chocolate (Lebbie's) | $2,000 | 6 months |\n| Software products/SaaS | $8,000 | 12-18 months |\n| Photography (selective) | $2,000 | Ongoing |\n| Investments (dividends) | $1,500 | Building |\n| Consulting/freelance | $1,500 | As needed |\n| **Total** | **$15,000** | |\n\n### Strategic synergies:\n- **Photography × Chocolate:** Doug shoots his own product photography (saves $$$, builds brand)\n- **Software × Chocolate:** Build tools for artisan food businesses (you ARE the customer)\n- **PIH Story × All:** The humanitarian narrative elevates everything — press, partnerships, community\n\n---\n\n## Key Risks & Mitigations\n\n| Risk | Mitigation |\n|---|---|\n| Perishability / shipping | Start local (markets + Baltimore DTC), expand shipping in cooler months |\n| Scaling production | Shared commercial kitchen → own space only when demand justifies |\n| Cocoa supply chain | Build relationships with 2-3 Sierra Leonean cooperatives |\n| Seasonality | Corporate gifting fills Q4; subscriptions smooth revenue |\n\n---\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nDoug has an **unfair advantage stack**: world-class training, a story nobody can copy, photography skills for marketing, software skills for e-commerce, and 5 years of relationships in cocoa country. The question isn't whether this can work — it's how fast to scale it.\n\n*Start small. Start now. The beans are waiting.*\n"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus-4-5","usage":{"input":1,"output":1837,"cacheRead":10925,"cacheWrite":149,"totalTokens":12912,"cost":{"input":0.000005,"output":0.045925,"cacheRead":0.0054624999999999995,"cacheWrite":0.00093125,"total":0.05232375}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1769916376102}}
